export default {
  "wt2-media-01": "It is commonly claimed that newspapers and television news outlets are more trustworthy than the information circulating on the internet. I largely agree with this view, since established media organisations are bound by professional standards, although I recognise that the internet can still be a valuable source when used with due caution.\n\nThe greater reliability of traditional media stems from the editorial processes behind them. Professional journalists are trained to verify facts, consult multiple sources and correct their mistakes, and they operate within a legal framework that punishes defamation and false reporting. A front-page article in a respected newspaper, for instance, has typically passed through several layers of editing and fact-checking before publication. Consequently, readers can reasonably expect a level of accuracy that is rarely matched online.\n\nBy contrast, the internet allows virtually anyone to publish content without scrutiny. Social media platforms are flooded with rumours, manipulated images and deliberately misleading headlines, which can reach millions of users within hours. Although certain websites, such as those of academic institutions, are highly reliable, the average user cannot easily distinguish verified information from propaganda. The speed at which unverified claims circulate online, often faster than corrections can be issued, makes the internet an inherently riskier source of news.\n\nThat said, it would be unfair to dismiss the internet entirely, as it offers instant access to breaking stories and a diversity of viewpoints that traditional outlets may overlook.\n\nIn conclusion, while both mediums have their merits, I maintain that newspapers and television remain more dependable sources of news than the internet, whose very openness is also its greatest weakness.",
  "wt2-media-02": "Celebrities, from pop stars to sports icons, are frequently looked up to by young people as role models. While this can occasionally inspire ambition and healthy habits, I believe that treating celebrities as models of behaviour is on balance a negative development for the younger generation.\n\nOn the one hand, some celebrities genuinely exert a constructive influence. Athletes such as professional footballers often demonstrate discipline, perseverance and teamwork, and their achievements can motivate teenagers to train hard and set goals for themselves. Likewise, musicians and actors who campaign for charitable causes or speak openly about mental health can help young fans develop empathy and social awareness. In these cases, admiration translates into positive aspirations rather than mere imitation.\n\nOn the other hand, the celebrity culture promoted by the media is largely built on image rather than substance. Many famous figures owe their status to appearance, wealth or scandal rather than to talent or moral character, and they are frequently photographed engaging in extravagant spending or reckless behaviour. Impressionable adolescents may copy such conduct, believing that fame justifies excess, and they may come to measure success by followers and likes instead of by effort and integrity. Moreover, the relentless pursuit of an unrealistic celebrity lifestyle can breed dissatisfaction with ordinary life and undermine young people's self-esteem.\n\nIn my view, the harm is amplified because young fans rarely encounter the ordinary human side of celebrities, such as their failures or the long years of struggle behind their success.\n\nIn conclusion, although a minority of celebrities set a good example, the overwhelming emphasis on glamour and spectacle makes the widespread idolisation of famous people a largely negative trend that parents and schools should help young people to view critically.",
  "wt2-media-03": "It is sometimes suggested that newspapers, television and social media should give more coverage to positive stories and less to disasters, scandals and conflicts. Although I appreciate the appeal of such a shift, I disagree that the media should focus predominantly on good news, because doing so would weaken their essential role in society.\n\nThere are understandable reasons for wanting more uplifting coverage. Constant exposure to violence, political turmoil and economic gloom can leave the public feeling anxious and powerless, and studies suggest that a steady diet of bad news damages mental wellbeing. Celebrating achievements, scientific breakthroughs and acts of kindness could raise morale, encourage generosity and give people a more balanced impression of the world.\n\nHowever, the primary duty of the press is to inform citizens rather than to comfort them. Journalists act as watchdogs who expose corruption, warn the public about dangers and hold those in power to account. If editors prioritised pleasant stories, serious problems such as pollution scandals, financial fraud or human rights abuses could go unnoticed until they spiralled out of control. Furthermore, a media landscape devoted to feel-good content would simply lose credibility, since readers would soon suspect that unpleasant facts were being concealed.\n\nIn my opinion, the solution is not to replace bad news with good news but to report both accurately and proportionately, allowing the audience to form its own judgement.\n\nIn conclusion, while journalists should certainly highlight progress and decency, I believe that slanting coverage towards good news would be a mistake, because an informed public depends on honest reporting of the full picture, however uncomfortable it may be.",
  "wt2-media-04": "Violence on screen is frequently held responsible for aggressive behaviour in society, and many campaigners call for stricter censorship of films and television programmes. While I accept that media violence can influence some viewers, I disagree that it is the main cause of violent conduct, which stems from far deeper social and psychological factors.\n\nIt would be naive to deny any connection between screen violence and real aggression. Young children, in particular, are still developing their understanding of right and wrong, and they may imitate what they see, especially when violence is glamorised and shown as an effective way of resolving conflict. Psychological experiments have found short-term increases in aggressive thoughts after exposure to violent material, and repeated viewing can gradually desensitise audiences to suffering. For vulnerable individuals, such content may lower the barriers that would otherwise restrain them.\n\nNevertheless, the overwhelming majority of people watch violent films without ever committing a crime, which suggests that media exposure alone cannot explain violent behaviour. Far more significant are factors such as poverty, unstable family backgrounds, unemployment, substance abuse and untreated mental illness, all of which are strongly correlated with offending. Violent behaviour also exists in societies where media are tightly controlled, indicating that its roots lie in social conditions rather than in entertainment.\n\nIn my view, therefore, governments should focus their efforts on tackling deprivation and improving support services rather than on blaming the entertainment industry.\n\nIn conclusion, although graphic violence on screen may reinforce aggressive tendencies in a minority of viewers, I believe that attributing society's violence to films and television oversimplifies a complex problem whose principal causes are social and economic.",
  "wt2-media-05": "Some people argue that reading books is a superior way of acquiring knowledge compared with watching television or using the internet. While each medium has distinct strengths, I believe that books remain unequalled for deep learning, even though digital sources offer clear advantages in speed and accessibility.\n\nThe case for books rests on the depth of engagement they demand. Reading requires sustained concentration and active interpretation, forcing the mind to construct meaning from words rather than passively receiving images. This process strengthens comprehension, vocabulary and critical thinking, and a well-written book can develop an argument in far greater detail than a television programme or a webpage. Academic knowledge, philosophy and history, for example, are still transmitted mainly through books precisely because complex ideas need the space that only print provides.\n\nHowever, it would be short-sighted to dismiss digital media. Television can convey vivid experiences that words struggle to capture, such as the spectacle of a volcanic eruption or the atmosphere of a foreign city, and documentaries make specialised subjects accessible to a wide audience. The internet, meanwhile, offers instant answers, up-to-date information and interactive resources, enabling learners to cross-check facts, follow their curiosity and study at their own pace. For practical skills such as learning a language or following breaking news, digital media are often more convenient and efficient than books.\n\nIn my view, the two approaches are complementary rather than competing, and the most effective learners combine them.\n\nIn conclusion, although television and the internet have transformed access to information, I maintain that books are still the most reliable route to genuine understanding, because they train the mind to think deeply rather than merely to absorb images.",
  "wt2-transport-01": "Traffic congestion has become one of the most intractable problems facing modern cities, and some authorities propose charging drivers a fee to enter city centres as a solution. I strongly agree with this measure, provided that it is accompanied by affordable alternatives to driving.\n\nThere are clear reasons why a congestion charge is effective. The first is economic: when a journey carries a visible cost, drivers are forced to weigh whether their trip is genuinely necessary, and many will switch to public transport, cycle or car-share. London, for example, introduced such a charge in 2003 and immediately recorded a significant fall in traffic levels and journey times, while revenue from the scheme was channelled into bus and tube improvements. In this way the policy attacks congestion from two directions at once, discouraging car use while funding better transport options.\n\nCritics, however, raise legitimate objections. They point out that the fee falls hardest on lower-income workers who have no practical alternative, and that some drivers simply pay it and carry on, so the effect can diminish over time. These concerns are valid, which is why the charge should be set at a sensible level and paired with expanded park-and-ride facilities, cheaper fares and safe cycle lanes, so that giving up the car is a realistic choice rather than an imposition.\n\nIn my opinion, the alternative to pricing is far worse: doing nothing guarantees ever-lengthening gridlock, wasted fuel and polluted air.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that charging drivers for entering city centres is one of the most practical tools available to tackle congestion, and its drawbacks can be managed through careful design and investment in public transport.",
  "wt2-transport-02": "In many cities buses and trains run half-empty while roads are clogged with private cars. Understanding the causes is the first step towards reversing this trend, and a combination of improved services and financial incentives is required.\n\nThe main causes of this situation are practical and psychological. For many commuters, the car is simply more convenient: it offers door-to-door travel, saves time spent waiting at stops, and allows people to carry shopping or children with ease. Public transport, by contrast, is often perceived as unreliable, infrequent or overcrowded, particularly in suburbs where services are thin. Cost also plays a part, since a car is frequently seen as cheaper than daily fares once it is owned, and comfort, privacy and status make driving attractive even when buses are available.\n\nThe solutions therefore need to make alternatives genuinely competitive. Firstly, frequency and punctuality must improve, because a bus that arrives every ten minutes is far more tempting than one that appears once an hour; dedicated bus lanes and priority signalling can deliver this without huge expense. Secondly, fares should be simplified and reduced, with integrated ticketing that allows a single card to cover buses, trains and trams. Thirdly, park-and-ride schemes and secure bicycle parking at stations encourage people to combine modes, while congestion charging and scarce, expensive parking make driving less attractive.\n\nIn my view, governments must also change attitudes, presenting public transport as a modern, comfortable choice rather than a fallback for those without cars.\n\nIn conclusion, the underuse of public transport stems largely from its failure to compete with the convenience of the car, but with reliable, affordable and well-integrated services, alongside disincentives for driving, the balance can be shifted in favour of buses and trains.",
  "wt2-transport-03": "Governments face a constant dilemma over transport spending: should scarce funds be poured into roads and motorways, or into railways and public transport? Both approaches have strong advocates, and in my view the wisest course is a balanced strategy that favours public transport for cities while recognising that roads remain essential.\n\nThose who favour road building point to the freedom and flexibility that cars provide, particularly in rural areas where rail links are uneconomic and buses are rare. Freight also depends overwhelmingly on lorries, and better roads reduce delivery times, cut fuel consumption and support businesses. Moreover, motorways and bypasses are seen as quick, visible achievements that ease congestion around towns and stimulate regional economies.\n\nOn the other hand, railways and public transport offer advantages that roads can never match. A single train carries the equivalent of hundreds of cars, so investment in rail reduces congestion and emissions far more efficiently than widening a motorway, which tends to fill up with new traffic within a few years. Modern electric trains also support denser, more sustainable cities, and reliable commuter services allow people to live without cars altogether. Evidence from countries such as Japan and the Netherlands shows that sustained investment in rail and cycling produces cleaner, more efficient transport systems than car-centric planning.\n\nIn my judgement, the two are not rivals but partners: the goal should be to shift short urban journeys to public transport while maintaining the roads that freight and rural communities depend on.\n\nIn conclusion, although roads will always be necessary, I believe that a greater share of public money should be redirected towards railways and public transport, because they offer the only sustainable answer to congestion and pollution in the long term.",
  "wt2-transport-04": "Car ownership has expanded dramatically over the past century, and some commentators argue that this boom has generated more problems than it has solved. I largely agree with this view, because the social and environmental costs of mass motorisation now threaten to outweigh the personal freedoms it delivers.\n\nThe benefits of the car should not be dismissed lightly. It provides door-to-door mobility, allowing people to commute to work, collect children and reach remote destinations at any hour, and for the disabled and elderly it is often the only realistic means of independent travel. Cars have also reshaped economies, enabling just-in-time delivery and the growth of suburbs and out-of-town shopping centres.\n\nNevertheless, the cumulative damage is severe. Widespread car use is a major source of air pollution and carbon emissions, contributing to respiratory illness and climate change, while traffic congestion wastes countless hours and billions in lost productivity every year. The dominance of the car has also shaped cities in harmful ways: neighbourhoods are divided by motorways, streets become unsafe for pedestrians and children, and the sedentary lifestyle that accompanies driving fuels obesity and heart disease. Furthermore, the huge toll of road deaths and injuries, many involving pedestrians and cyclists, is a price that society too readily accepts.\n\nIn my opinion, the decisive point is that most of these costs are borne by society rather than by drivers themselves, which is why the balance has tipped so far towards the negative.\n\nIn conclusion, although cars offer undeniable convenience, I believe that their proliferation has created more problems than it has solved, and the priority now must be to promote walking, cycling and public transport while restraining further growth in private car use.",
  "wt2-transport-05": "It is often claimed that encouraging people to walk or cycle instead of driving would solve most of the transport problems facing cities. While active travel can certainly make a major contribution, I disagree that it alone would resolve the issue, because distance, climate and the needs of particular groups make cycling and walking impractical in many situations.\n\nThere is much to support the argument. Bicycles and pedestrians occupy far less space than cars, so promoting them eases congestion, reduces emissions and improves air quality, while regular exercise cuts the risk of heart disease and obesity. Cities such as Copenhagen and Amsterdam demonstrate that comprehensive cycle networks, combined with safe crossings and parking, can shift a large share of journeys away from cars and make streets calmer and more pleasant.\n\nHowever, the limits of this approach are clear. Modern cities have sprawled, and many commuters travel ten or twenty kilometres from suburbs to city centres, distances that are simply too far for most people to cycle daily. Heavy rain, extreme heat and cold winters discourage cycling in many climates, and the elderly, the disabled and parents carrying young children cannot reasonably be expected to ride. Expecting active travel to solve everything therefore places an unfair burden on individuals while ignoring structural realities.\n\nIn my view, walking and cycling should be a central pillar of transport policy, but they must be combined with reliable public transport and targeted road pricing.\n\nIn conclusion, although promoting cycling and walking is highly beneficial and should be pursued vigorously, I believe that claiming it would solve most transport problems overstates the case, since many journeys and citizens will always require motorised alternatives.",
  "wt2-culture-01": "Globalisation has transformed the way people live, work and communicate, and its effect on culture is hotly debated. Some fear that it is erasing traditional cultures, while others celebrate the way it brings different societies closer together. In my view, both positions contain truth, but the outcome depends largely on how nations respond.\n\nThe case for cultural loss is compelling. Multinational corporations export the same brands, films and fast-food chains to every corner of the globe, so that young people in vastly different countries wear the same clothes, listen to the same music and adopt similar lifestyles. Local languages, crafts and customs can be crowded out when global products dominate the market, and communities may abandon their own traditions in pursuit of a more 'modern' identity. Small cinemas showing domestic films, for instance, frequently close because they cannot compete with Hollywood blockbusters.\n\nAt the same time, globalisation undoubtedly fosters cultural exchange. Migrants carry their cuisines, festivals and art across borders, so that cities like London or Singapore become vibrant mosaics of world cultures. People can now learn about distant traditions through the internet, travel and international media, and cross-cultural collaborations produce new hybrid art forms, from fusion cooking to world music. In this sense, globalisation enriches our cultural horizons rather than merely flattening them.\n\nIn my opinion, the real issue is passivity: cultures do not disappear automatically but are weakened when governments and communities fail to support them. Where heritage is actively protected and taught, as in Japan or India, globalisation and tradition coexist successfully.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that globalisation brings cultures closer together while also threatening their distinctiveness, and the challenge is to embrace exchange without surrendering the unique identities that give each society its character.",
  "wt2-culture-02": "Around the world, traditional customs and minority languages are vanishing at an alarming rate, with many linguists predicting that thousands of dialects will disappear within a generation. I regard this as a distinctly negative development, because the loss is irreversible and diminishes both cultural diversity and the identity of the communities concerned.\n\nOne reason for concern is that customs and languages are repositories of accumulated wisdom. Indigenous knowledge of plants, medicine, weather and the natural environment is frequently embedded in local languages and passed down through rituals, songs and stories, and when a language dies, this knowledge dies with it. Similarly, festivals, ceremonies and crafts connect people to their history and give them a sense of belonging, so their disappearance can leave communities feeling rootless and disconnected from their ancestors.\n\nMoreover, the erosion of diversity impoverishes everyone, not only the affected groups. The world's cultures are its shared heritage, comparable to its biological species: each tradition offers a unique way of seeing and interpreting human experience, and as they disappear, our collective imagination narrows.\n\nIt could be argued that change is natural and that no culture remains static, and that adopting new customs is simply part of survival in a globalised world. Yet this argument overlooks the fact that much of the decline is not voluntary adaptation but forced neglect, driven by global media, migration and the dominance of a few major languages.\n\nIn conclusion, while some cultural change is inevitable and even healthy, I believe that the rapid disappearance of traditional customs and languages is a negative development, and that governments, schools and communities should make determined efforts to document and revive them before they are lost forever.",
  "wt2-culture-03": "The relationship between international tourism and local culture is a source of lasting debate, with some arguing that visitors destroy the traditions they come to admire, while others insist that tourism actually helps preserve them. Both views have merit, and in my opinion the outcome depends on how tourism is managed.\n\nThose who condemn tourism point to the commodification of culture. When a village festival is staged for paying visitors, it can lose its spiritual meaning and become a performance, and locals may dress, dance or cook in ways designed to please tourists rather than to express their own beliefs. Ancient sites suffer from overcrowding, and young people may abandon traditional crafts in favour of more lucrative souvenir production.\n\nConversely, tourism can be a powerful force for preservation. Revenue from entrance fees and guided tours is frequently used to restore monuments, maintain historic quarters and fund traditional artisans, who otherwise could not earn a living from their skills. International interest also generates pride: when outsiders value a community's music, food or language, local people are encouraged to keep these practices alive and pass them on. Countries such as Peru and Bhutan demonstrate that well-regulated tourism can support heritage rather than erode it.\n\nIn my judgement, the key variable is regulation and local involvement. Where communities own the tourism industry, set limits on visitor numbers and educate travellers about respectful behaviour, the benefits are widely shared; where outsiders control it purely for profit, damage is almost inevitable.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that international tourism damages local cultures when it is unregulated and exploitative, but that it genuinely helps preserve them when it is planned sensitively with local people at the centre.",
  "wt2-culture-04": "Every government faces hard choices about how to allocate limited public funds, and the debate between preserving historical buildings and building new housing and infrastructure is a classic example. While I appreciate the cultural value of heritage, I believe the answer lies in a balanced approach that weighs both claims case by case.\n\nThe argument for preserving historical buildings is powerful. Landmarks such as castles, cathedrals and old town centres are irreplaceable witnesses to a nation's history, and once demolished they can never be rebuilt authentically. They attract tourists, generate employment and give citizens a tangible sense of identity and continuity. Moreover, careful restoration can be more sustainable than demolition and rebuilding, since old structures embody materials and craftsmanship that simply cannot be replicated.\n\nOn the other hand, the case for new housing and infrastructure is equally pressing. In many countries, soaring property prices and homelessness mean that families desperately need affordable homes, and modern hospitals, schools and roads are essential for a functioning society. Keeping every old building untouched can distort urban development, forcing new construction into the countryside. Where a building is of little architectural merit, or where its maintenance costs exceed its value, demolition may be the more responsible use of money.\n\nIn my judgement, the sensible policy is to protect buildings of genuine significance while allowing ordinary, undistinguished structures to make way for development.\n\nIn conclusion, although heritage preservation deserves generous support, I believe that governments should balance it against pressing social needs, reserving the strongest protection for the most valuable buildings rather than treating every old structure as sacred.",
  "wt2-culture-05": "Food is often described as the heart of a nation's culture, and some people argue that citizens should therefore eat their own traditional dishes rather than foreign food. While I understand the emotional appeal of this view, I disagree that people should restrict themselves to local cuisine, because culinary exchange enriches rather than diminishes a culture.\n\nIt is true that traditional food carries deep meaning. Dishes are linked to history, geography and family memory, and sharing a national meal can strengthen community bonds and pass values from one generation to the next. Festivals, weddings and religious ceremonies are often inseparable from particular foods, and mass-produced global chains do threaten small local restaurants and artisanal producers.\n\nNevertheless, the idea that people should eat only their own food is based on a misunderstanding of how cultures develop. Every national cuisine is itself a product of past exchange: the tomato, now central to Italian cooking, came from the Americas, and tea and spices transformed British and Indian kitchens alike. Welcoming foreign food does not erase tradition but adds to it, as the global popularity of pizza and sushi shows. Furthermore, immigrants bring their food with them, and the resulting fusion dishes are one of the most vivid expressions of a multicultural society.\n\nIn my view, the real threat to traditional food is not foreign cuisine itself but the dominance of cheap, uniform fast food, and the appropriate response is to support local producers and teach cooking skills rather than to forbid other cuisines.\n\nIn conclusion, although traditional food deserves protection and pride, I believe that people should be free to enjoy a wide variety of cuisines, since openness to foreign food strengthens culture instead of destroying it.",
  "wt2-family-01": "In many households today, both parents work full time, leaving comparatively little time for their children. While this arrangement is often an economic necessity, I believe that on balance it is a negative development, because of the toll it takes on children and family life.\n\nThere are, of course, respectable reasons why dual-career families have become the norm. In most countries two salaries are now required simply to meet the cost of housing, education and daily living, and for many families a stay-at-home parent is an unaffordable luxury. Working parents also provide their children with material advantages, such as better schools and extracurricular activities, and a mother or father who is fulfilled professionally may well be a more patient and stimulating parent at home.\n\nNevertheless, the costs are considerable. Children who spend long hours in childcare or alone may miss out on the guidance and emotional security that attentive parenting provides, and research links parental absence with behavioural problems and weaker academic motivation. When both parents return home exhausted, mealtimes are rushed, homework is checked hastily, and conversation is replaced by screens, so family bonds gradually loosen. Moreover, the stress of juggling deadlines and childcare often leaves parents guilty and overstretched.\n\nIn my view, the problem is not that mothers work but that working patterns are so rigid; flexible hours and shared parental leave could allow both parents to pursue careers without sacrificing family life.\n\nIn conclusion, although financial pressures make dual-earner families understandable, I believe that the shortage of parental time is a negative development that harms children and weakens families, and that governments and employers should do more to help parents combine work and care.",
  "wt2-family-02": "There is ongoing disagreement about how children should be brought up, with some parents favouring firm discipline and others preferring a more relaxed, permissive approach. Both philosophies have strengths and weaknesses, and in my view the most effective parenting combines the two rather than adopting either extreme.\n\nThe case for strict discipline rests on the belief that children need clear boundaries. Firm rules teach respect, self-control and responsibility, and consistent consequences help young people understand that actions have results, which prepares them for the demands of school and adult life. Without guidance, it is argued, children may become demanding, disrespectful or unable to cope with frustration, and permissive homes are often blamed for producing teenagers who struggle with authority and commitment.\n\nOn the other hand, an overly rigid approach carries its own dangers. Children who are constantly punished and controlled may become anxious, resentful or secretive, and they may rebel as soon as they gain independence. Harsh discipline can also damage the trust between parent and child, so that the child stops seeking advice precisely when it is most needed. By contrast, relaxed parenting that treats children as individuals, explains reasons and allows mistakes tends to build confidence, creativity and open communication.\n\nIn my judgement, the key is the quality of the relationship rather than the strictness of the rules. Authoritative parents, who set firm expectations but explain them warmly and listen to their children's views, generally achieve the best outcomes, as decades of research have shown.\n\nIn conclusion, both strict and relaxed approaches have merit, but I believe that the wisest course is a balanced style that provides clear boundaries within a supportive, communicative relationship, adapting to the age and temperament of each child.",
  "wt2-family-03": "Modern children spend increasing amounts of time with smartphones, tablets and games consoles, often at the expense of time with their families. This trend creates a range of problems, and tackling them requires a combination of household rules, parental engagement and sensible use of technology.\n\nThe problems are multifaceted. Physically, excessive screen time is linked to poor posture, eye strain and, above all, inactivity, which contributes to childhood obesity; late-night device use also disrupts sleep and concentration at school. Socially, children who immerse themselves in virtual worlds may develop weaker face-to-face communication skills, finding it harder to read emotions, share feelings or resolve arguments, while heavy use of social media can expose them to cyberbullying and unrealistic comparisons that damage self-esteem. Within the family itself, the device becomes a barrier: meals are eaten in silence, outings are punctuated by scrolling, and parents and children gradually inhabit separate worlds.\n\nThe solutions begin at home. Parents should set clear and consistent limits, such as no screens at mealtimes or after a certain hour, and, crucially, they should model the behaviour they expect by putting away their own phones. Equally important is offering attractive alternatives: shared board games, sports, cooking and family outings can compete with screens far more effectively than simple prohibition. Schools can contribute by teaching digital literacy and healthy habits.\n\nIn my opinion, the aim should not be to ban technology, which is impossible and even counterproductive, but to make its use deliberate rather than automatic.\n\nIn conclusion, the growing dominance of electronic devices in children's lives causes real damage to their health, social skills and family bonds, yet these problems are manageable through consistent limits, enthusiastic alternatives and the example set by parents themselves.",
  "wt2-family-04": "The role of grandparents in bringing up children is a subject of some debate: some believe they should be more involved, while others insist that childcare is primarily the parents' responsibility. In my view, parents should bear the main responsibility, but grandparents can play a valuable and enriching supporting role.\n\nThose who favour a greater role for grandparents point to the benefits of their experience and availability. Grandparents have already raised children, so they bring practical wisdom, patience and a calmer perspective that first-time parents often lack, and where both parents work, grandparents provide affordable, trustworthy childcare that is far more flexible than nurseries. Moreover, a close bond with grandparents enriches children emotionally, connecting them to family history, traditions and stories.\n\nNevertheless, there are strong reasons why parents should remain the primary carers. Raising a child is a personal responsibility that parents take on when they decide to have children, and the daily decisions about health, education and values should ultimately be theirs. If grandparents take charge, conflicts are common, since generations often disagree about discipline and diet. There is also a risk of placing an unfair burden on older relatives, who may be frail or entitled to enjoy their later years without full-time childcare duties.\n\nIn my judgement, the ideal arrangement is a partnership in which grandparents help regularly but parents retain control of major decisions; this gives children love and stability from both generations without blurring lines of authority.\n\nIn conclusion, although grandparents can contribute enormously to children's upbringing through care, experience and affection, I believe that parents should carry the main responsibility, with grandparents offering support that enriches rather than replaces the parental role.",
  "wt2-family-05": "It is widely observed that families today are smaller and less close-knit than in previous generations, with fewer children, distant relatives and less shared time. While this change brings certain freedoms, I believe it is on the whole a negative development, because the emotional and practical support that extended families provide is hard to replace.\n\nSome benefits of the modern family should be acknowledged. Smaller families allow parents to invest more attention, money and opportunities in each child, and households with fewer members tend to enjoy greater privacy, flexibility and independence. Geographic mobility, which separates families, also brings career opportunities and personal freedom, and many people prefer the autonomy of choosing their own lifestyle without constant family scrutiny. In this sense, the loosening of family ties is partly the price of progress.\n\nHowever, the costs are substantial. The extended family traditionally served as a safety net: grandparents helped with childcare, relatives supported the elderly, and cousins provided companionship and a sense of belonging. When families shrink and scatter, these functions fall on the state or on isolated individuals, and caring for ageing parents or young children becomes a lonely struggle. Emotionally, people with weaker family bonds report higher levels of loneliness and stress.\n\nMoreover, modern pressures magnify the problem: both parents working, digital distractions and long commutes leave even small families with little genuine time together, so the family that remains is often more fragmented than ever.\n\nIn conclusion, although smaller, less connected families offer advantages in terms of resources and freedom, I believe that the erosion of family closeness is a negative development that leaves individuals more isolated and less supported, and that making deliberate efforts to maintain contact and shared rituals is more important than ever.",
  "wt2-consumption-01": "Consumerism, the relentless desire to acquire ever more goods, has become a defining feature of modern societies. While it has undoubtedly driven economic growth, I agree with those who argue that, on balance, it is harming society through its effects on personal wellbeing, social values and the environment.\n\nThe economic case for consumer spending is straightforward. Demand for goods creates jobs, sustains businesses and generates tax revenue, and rising consumption has lifted millions of people out of poverty in developing countries. There is nothing inherently wrong with buying things, and the pleasures of a comfortable home or a well-made product are real. For this reason, calls to curb consumerism are sometimes dismissed as unrealistic nostalgia.\n\nYet the evidence of harm is difficult to ignore. At the individual level, the pursuit of possessions frequently fails to deliver happiness: people adapt quickly to new purchases, so the satisfaction fades and the cycle of wanting begins again, leaving many consumers trapped in debt and chronic dissatisfaction. At the social level, materialism encourages people to judge themselves and others by wealth, weakening community values and fuelling envy, while advertising creates artificial needs and pressures, especially among the young. Most seriously, the environmental cost is unsustainable: mass production and disposal consume finite resources, generate mountains of waste and drive the carbon emissions that are heating the planet.\n\nIn my opinion, the problem is not consumption itself but its excess, and societies would benefit from shifting emphasis towards experiences and durability rather than endless acquisition.\n\nIn conclusion, although consumerism has contributed to prosperity, I believe that its negative consequences for mental wellbeing, social cohesion and the environment now outweigh its benefits, and that a more thoughtful, sustainable approach to consumption is urgently needed.",
  "wt2-consumption-02": "In many countries people now purchase far more goods than they genuinely need, a trend driven by advertising, social pressure and easy credit. This overconsumption causes serious problems for individuals and the planet, and it will only be reversed through a combination of awareness, regulation and personal restraint.\n\nThe reasons for this behaviour are varied. Most obviously, advertising and marketing are designed to create desires rather than to satisfy needs: products are wrapped in images of happiness and status, and seasonal campaigns persuade people that they need the latest phone or fashion item. Social comparison amplifies the effect, as new purchases are displayed on social media. In addition, the spread of credit cards and buy-now-pay-later schemes has removed the natural brake of saving, allowing people to spend money they do not yet have.\n\nThe consequences are damaging on several fronts. Financially, households accumulate debt, and repayment stress takes a heavy toll on mental health. Environmentally, the constant production and disposal of goods consumes energy, depletes raw materials and produces vast amounts of waste. Socially, the culture of excess can crowd out more meaningful pursuits, as people measure success by possessions and find that the satisfaction of each new purchase fades quickly, prompting another round of spending.\n\nIn my view, the most promising solutions combine education that teaches financial literacy and the value of sufficiency, with policies such as durability standards, repair subsidies and restrictions on aggressive marketing to children.\n\nIn conclusion, excessive buying is driven by powerful commercial and social forces, but its costs in debt, waste and environmental damage are too high to ignore, and both individuals and governments need to take deliberate steps to consume more wisely.",
  "wt2-consumption-03": "It is often argued that buying local products is better for the economy and the environment than purchasing imported goods. I largely agree with this view, although I recognise that imports also have legitimate advantages and that the issue is more complex than a simple slogan.\n\nThe benefits of buying local are considerable. Economically, money spent on local goods circulates within the community, supporting farmers, artisans and small businesses, and helping to sustain local employment; in contrast, money spent on imports flows abroad, often to countries with far lower labour costs. Environmentally, locally produced goods require far less transport, so they generate fewer emissions from ships, planes and lorries. Supporting local producers also preserves skills, breeds and landscapes.\n\nHowever, the argument should not be pushed too far. Imports allow consumers to enjoy products that cannot be produced locally, such as tropical fruit or specialised machinery, and international trade lifts millions of people in developing countries out of poverty by giving them access to global markets. A blanket preference for local goods could therefore be protectionist and could raise prices, particularly for lower-income households, while some 'local' products are actually less efficient in energy and water use than imported ones. The choice is rarely between clearly good and clearly bad options.\n\nIn my opinion, the sensible approach is to favour local products where quality and price are comparable, while accepting that imports are indispensable for goods that cannot reasonably be made at home.\n\nIn conclusion, although buying local generally benefits both the domestic economy and the environment, I believe the wisest policy is balance, supporting local producers wherever possible without denying the genuine advantages of international trade.",
  "wt2-consumption-04": "Small independent shops are rapidly disappearing from high streets as large shopping malls and online stores take their place. While this shift offers consumers obvious conveniences, I believe that its disadvantages outweigh the advantages, because of the damage it inflicts on communities and employment.\n\nThe advantages of the trend are not hard to identify. Malls and e-commerce platforms offer an immense variety of goods under one roof or at a single click, usually at lower prices, since big chains benefit from economies of scale. Online shopping is open around the clock, allows easy price comparison and delivers to the door. For the economy, these businesses create thousands of jobs and attract investment.\n\nNevertheless, the losses are serious. When local shops close, high streets lose their character and become rows of empty windows, and residents lose walkable access to everyday goods, which particularly affects older people. Small shops are also major employers and training grounds for local people, and their decline concentrates wealth in distant corporations that contribute little to the community. Environmentally, the trend is costly too: customers drive further to malls. Perhaps most importantly, local shops are social spaces where neighbours meet and chat, and their disappearance erodes the sense of community that makes a town a home.\n\nIn my view, the two forms of retail can coexist, provided planners protect local shopping streets, support small businesses with lower rents and rates, and require large developers to contribute to the surrounding community.\n\nIn conclusion, although malls and online stores are undeniably convenient, I believe that the erosion of small local shops is a negative development whose social and economic costs outweigh its benefits, and that positive action is needed to keep high streets alive.",
  "wt2-consumption-05": "Consumers constantly face the choice between buying expensive products built to last and purchasing cheap alternatives that must be replaced frequently. While cheap goods are attractive to those on tight budgets, I believe that, whenever possible, buying quality products is the wiser choice, because they offer better long-term value and cause far less harm to the environment.\n\nThe appeal of cheap products is understandable. Low prices make goods accessible to people with limited incomes, allowing them to own phones, clothing or furniture they could otherwise never afford. Rapidly advancing technology also undermines the logic of durability, since an expensive laptop may become obsolete just as quickly as a budget one. In these cases, paying a premium is simply waste.\n\nHowever, the disadvantages of cheap goods are substantial. A product that fails after a few months is rarely a bargain: the consumer pays again and again, and the total cost over a decade frequently exceeds that of one well-made item. Quality products also tend to perform better and give more satisfaction while they last. Environmentally, the difference is stark: the manufacture of every product consumes energy and raw materials, so an endless cycle of cheap purchases multiplies pollution and waste, whereas buying once and keeping it for years is the most effective form of recycling.\n\nIn my opinion, the key is to buy quality selectively, investing in items used daily, such as shoes, cookware or furniture, while accepting disposability for genuinely one-off purchases.\n\nIn conclusion, although cheap products serve a real purpose for low-income households and occasional use, I believe that quality products that last are generally superior, offering better value in the long run and a significantly lighter footprint on the environment.",
  "wt2-government-01": "Governments are continually deciding how to divide limited budgets, and one recurring debate concerns spending on public services such as health and education versus spending on the arts. While the arts enrich society in valuable ways, I agree that public services must take priority when funds are scarce.\n\nThe case for the arts is genuine. Museums, theatres, orchestras and galleries preserve a nation's heritage, stimulate creativity and attract tourists, and they contribute to education, wellbeing and the economy through the jobs they support. A society that neglects its arts risks becoming materially rich but spiritually poor. For these reasons, the arts should never be dismissed as a luxury.\n\nNevertheless, the claims of health and education are far more urgent. A hospital that cannot treat patients or a school that cannot afford teachers is a direct and immediate threat to human welfare, whereas the absence of a new art gallery is merely disappointing. Health services save lives and relieve suffering, and education determines the skills and opportunities of the next generation; both have consequences that the arts cannot match. Moreover, people who are healthy and well educated are precisely those who can best appreciate and fund the arts, so spending on public services indirectly supports cultural life in the long term.\n\nIn my opinion, the two are not rivals but partners in a sensible order of priority: governments should fund the arts generously once core services are secure, rather than treating them as equal claimants on every budget.\n\nIn conclusion, although the arts deserve support for their cultural and economic contribution, I believe that health and education must always come first when public money is limited, because they address fundamental needs on which every other achievement depends.",
  "wt2-government-02": "Space exploration absorbs vast sums of public money, and many argue that these funds should instead be spent on solving problems on Earth. While both positions have merit, I believe that space programmes remain worthwhile, but that they should be pursued alongside, rather than at the expense of, urgent earthly needs.\n\nThose who defend space exploration point to its extraordinary returns. Satellite technology, for example, underpins weather forecasting, global communications, navigation and disaster monitoring, and countless everyday technologies, from memory foam to medical imaging, have their origins in space research. The scientific knowledge gained about our planet, the solar system and the universe is priceless, and ambitious projects inspire young people to study science and engineering. In the long term, understanding climate and exploring resources beyond Earth may even help secure humanity's future.\n\nYet the critics' case is difficult to dismiss. Millions of people still lack clean water, adequate healthcare and basic education, and it seems morally questionable to spend billions on a rover on Mars while children starve in poverty. Money devoted to space is money not available for hospitals, schools and housing, and the benefits of space research, although real, often arrive slowly and indirectly, whereas investment in health and education produces measurable improvements within years.\n\nIn my judgement, the answer is proportion rather than either-or. Space agencies should focus on programmes with clear practical benefits and collaborate internationally to share costs, while governments should ensure that earthly priorities are properly funded first.\n\nIn conclusion, although the demands of poverty and disease are urgent, I believe that space exploration still deserves public support because of its technological and inspirational benefits, provided it is funded sensibly and never allowed to displace essential spending on problems at home.",
  "wt2-government-03": "Some economists and politicians propose that governments should pay every citizen a basic income. Although this idea has considerable appeal as a remedy for poverty, I disagree that it should be implemented unconditionally, because the cost, the effects on work incentives and the risk of inflation are simply too serious.\n\nThe attractions of a universal basic income are real. It would eliminate extreme poverty and reduce inequality, providing a safety net for the unemployed, the disabled and carers whose work society never pays for. In an age of automation, a guaranteed income could cushion the transition and free people to study, care for relatives or start businesses.\n\nNevertheless, the practical objections are formidable. First, the cost would be astronomical: paying every adult even a modest sum would consume a large share of national budgets, forcing higher taxes or cuts to public spending. Second, a payment that is not conditional on work may weaken the incentive to seek employment, and some people might withdraw from the labour market. Third, if the income is added to existing wages rather than replacing them, it could fuel inflation, pushing up the very purchasing power it was meant to guarantee.\n\nIn my opinion, a more targeted approach is preferable: strengthening benefits for the genuinely needy, investing in training and job creation, and supporting families with children, which delivers similar security at a fraction of the cost.\n\nIn conclusion, although a universal basic income is an appealing idea in principle, I believe that its prohibitive expense and the risks to employment and prices make it impractical, and that resources are better directed towards those who truly need help.",
  "wt2-government-04": "The internet has become the world's central forum for information, and governments now face the question of whether they should restrict what can be published online. Both absolute freedom and blanket censorship are dangerous, and in my view the right approach is limited regulation that targets clearly harmful content while preserving free expression.\n\nThose who oppose any restriction invoke the fundamental principle of free speech. The internet has given a voice to ordinary citizens, exposing corruption, mobilising dissent and allowing people in authoritarian states to share information that traditional media suppress. Open debate is essential to democracy and scientific progress, and any law allowing governments to remove content could easily be abused to silence critics and manipulate public opinion. History shows that censorship rarely stops at the obviously harmful.\n\nOn the other hand, a completely unregulated internet has proved untenable. Platforms are flooded with false information that undermines public health and with hate speech and terrorist propaganda that incite real violence. Illegal material such as child abuse imagery, fraud and piracy also circulates freely, and governments have a duty to protect their citizens from such harm. Since private companies cannot be trusted to police themselves consistently, some statutory framework is unavoidable.\n\nIn my judgement, the solution is a clear line drawn in law: governments should be able to require the removal of content that is illegal or demonstrably harmful, through transparent and appealable processes, but they must not be allowed to block lawful speech or suppress political dissent.\n\nIn conclusion, while the internet's openness is precious and censorship is rightly feared, I believe that targeted regulation of genuinely harmful content is both necessary and compatible with freedom, provided it is limited, transparent and subject to independent oversight.",
  "wt2-government-05": "It is often claimed that countries should join forces to tackle global problems such as climate change, whereas others believe that each nation should attend to its own domestic issues first. While national priorities cannot be ignored, I believe that international cooperation is essential, because the most serious threats facing humanity simply do not respect borders.\n\nThe case for focusing on national issues is understandable. Governments are accountable to their own voters, who expect immediate improvements in jobs, health and housing, and public resources are always limited. It is also true that international agreements can be frustratingly slow and toothless, since every country is reluctant to sacrifice its own interests.\n\nHowever, this argument overlooks the nature of modern crises. Climate change, pandemics and financial instability are global in origin and effect: emissions from one continent warm the whole planet, and a virus can travel around the world in a matter of days. No single country can solve such problems alone, and those that try will simply be overwhelmed by events beyond their borders. Moreover, cooperation brings economies of scale and shared expertise, making solutions cheaper and faster, whereas competition and duplication waste both money and time.\n\nIn my view, the two approaches are not mutually exclusive. Countries must, of course, provide for their own citizens, but they should pursue international action on global threats as an extension of national self-interest, since investing in shared solutions ultimately protects one's own population.\n\nIn conclusion, although every government must answer to its own people first, I believe that working together on problems such as climate change is a necessity rather than a luxury, and that isolationism would leave every nation more vulnerable, not less."
};
